<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Seven Years' War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a>–<a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nordic Seven Years' War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern Seven Years' War was fought from 1563 to 1570 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> under Eric XIV and John III and an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Fought on land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and at sea in the Baltic, it ended in exhaustion with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1570">Treaty of Stettin</a> in 1570. No territory changed hands, but Sweden had to pay a large ransom to recover the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war.jpg" alt="Northern Seven Years' War" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LocalF15 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Seven_Years%27_War_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> lasted on and off from 1397 to 1523 and collapsed after Swedish resentment of Danish domination. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>, who claimed the Swedish throne in June 1523, Sweden began to rival Denmark–Norway, but its access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> was limited to a short stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a>, and Denmark controlled the Baltic. In 1544 Gustav Vasa used the old Scandinavian levy to establish one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first native standing armies.</p><p>After the deaths of Christian III in 1559 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> in 1560, both realms had young monarchs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a> envisioned reviving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> under Danish leadership, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV of Sweden</a> wanted to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s dominant position. Disputes over the Three Crowns insignia and Eric's failed marriage negotiations in 1563 added to the tension. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, upset by Eric's obstacles to the Russian trade, joined Denmark, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> joined to gain control of Baltic trade. War was declared on 13 August 1563.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a> with about 25,000 professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> fortress on 4 September 1563 after three days of bombardment, cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Later, the Danish commander Daniel Rantzau defeated the Swedish army at the Battle of Axtorna on 20 October 1565. The Swedes fared better at sea, where the admiral Klas Horn destroyed much of a Danish–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> fleet, levied a toll on ships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Öresund</a> and secured the eastern Baltic in 1565.</p><p>In the north, Swedish forces occupied Jämtland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harjedalen">Härjedalen</a> and Trøndelag, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>, but were repelled from Bergenhus. A Swedish column in 1567 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar">Hamar</a>, reached the Skiensfjord and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skien">Skien</a> before being repelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. The Danish army, though superior, would not fight until its pay was current. Eric turned insane, paralysing the Swedish effort. In 1568 his brother John staged a coup and was enthroned as John III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John III opened negotiations, and a draft peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> on 18 November 1568 was rejected by the Swedes, so fighting resumed in 1569. With both armies exhausted, a meeting began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> in September 1570, and peace was reached on 13 December 1570 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1570">Treaty of Stettin</a>. The Swedish king withdrew claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, Skåne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a> and Gotland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> withdrew claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> was declared dissolved. Sweden ransomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> for 150,000 riksdaler and returned captured warships. The Three Crowns dispute stayed unresolved. Historians disagree on whether the war ended in a Danish victory or inconclusively.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern Seven Years' War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
